  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe thunderstorms and some instances of \nflash flooding are possible on Sunday.\n\nSunday is shaping up to be an active weather day across the region. \nShowers and embedded strong to severe thunderstorms are noted on\nradar near Columbus, Ohio and back into northwestern Ohio. This\nactivity will continue to move toward the east-southeast over\nthe next several hours, crossing Ohio, and moving into northern\nWest Virginia and southwest Pennsylvania a few hours before\ndaybreak. A couple of these showers could be heavy and reach\nwestern Maryland and parts of northeastern West Virginia in our\nforecast area around daybreak or shortly before. The main threat\nwill be brief gusty winds and heavy rain. As for the remainder\nof Sunday into Sunday evening, additional rounds of showers and\nstrong to severe thunderstorms could develop with multiple waves\nof mid-level energy. The main threats will be damaging wind\ngusts, locally heavy rainfall that could lead to localized flash\nflooding, and a possible tornado or two. In perspective to\nsurface boundary relative to the mid-level waves of energy, a\nwarm front will push northward Sunday morning to midday, before\nan area of low pressure connects to it, followed by a cold\nfront. This trifecta of synoptic features and boundaries will\njust enhance the possibility of severe weather Sunday and Sunday\nnight. The Storms Prediction Center has an area from Cumberland,\nMaryland to Manassas, Virginia to St. Mary's Maryland and all\nareas to the southwest of this line in a Slight Risk of Severe\nThunderstorms. All areas to the northeast of this line is in a\nMarginal Risk. The Weather Prediction Center has Garrett County\nand parts of western Grant and Pendleton County in a Slight Risk\nfor Excessive Rainfall. Areas to the east and up to US 15 is in\na Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall. A Flood Watch remains in\neffect for Garrett County in western Maryland. This is effect\nfrom 6am to Midnight through the day Sunday into Sunday evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable conditions continue through the week with \na break in thunderstorm chances Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nWinds will shift west or northwest by Monday, but the actual cold \nfront/moisture gradient will be lagging in the Ohio Valley. It's \npossible a few showers and thunderstorms form Monday as the front \nmoves through, although the downsloping flow and modest mid level \nlapse rates will likely limit coverage. Above normal temperatures \nand humid conditions will continue until the front clears the area \nMonday night.\n\nUpper-level zonal flow bringing drier air from the Great Lakes will \nbe in place Tuesday and Wednesday, creating a break in the rain and \nthunderstorms. Temperatures and humidity levels will drop a bit but \nremain near seasonable averages.\n\nMeanwhile, an upper-level trough will begin to form over the \nNorthern Plains and then track across the Great Lakes late in the \nweek, with a strong cold front potentially approaching the Mid-\nAtlantic between Thursday and Friday. This front could even stall \nacross the area into next weekend, bringing persistent chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms. At this time, it's unclear if the \nposition of synoptic features and instability will be sufficient for \na threat of severe weather or flooding. For now, this will be a \nfeature to continue to monitor as the synoptic setup becomes more \ndefined. Temperatures may fluctuate a little depending on what side \nof the front we're on, but likely fall some by next weekend assuming \nthe front pushes to the southeast.",
